<body>
    <h1>AI-356: AI+ System TLS/mTLS Implementation Guide</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p></p>
<p>This guide documents our approach to implementing Transport Layer Securi=
ty (TLS) and mutual TLS (mTLS) across the AI+ system. We'll be securing com=
munications between all components in our architecture, including the Notif=
icationAPI gateway, microservices (Metagen, VisageAI, ObjectRecognition, et=
c.), vector databases (Chroma, Milvus), and Hashicorp tools (Consul, Vault)=
. Per corporate requirements, we're using Let's Encrypt certificates for al=
l TLS implementations.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-ArchitectureStrategy"=
>Architecture Strategy</h2>
<p>Our TLS implementation follows a phased approach:</p>
<p>Phase 1: Let's Encrypt with Shared Certificates</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Single certificate with Subject Alternative Names (SANs) for all service=
s</p></li>
<li>
<p>Filesystem-based certificate storage</p></li>
<li>
<p>Automated renewal via certbot</p></li>
<li>
<p>Simple certificate distribution through file system</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Phase 2: Vault-based PKI Implementation</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Vault as certificate authority</p></li>
<li>
<p>Dynamic certificate generation</p></li>
<li>
<p>Service-specific certificates with shorter lifetimes</p></li>
<li>
<p>Automated rotation handled by Vault</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-DomainStructure">Doma=
in Structure</h2>
<p>For our internal systems, we're using a subdomain structure under <a cla=
ss=3D"external-link" href=3D"http://aiplus.perifery.com" rel=3D"nofollow">a=
iplus.perifery.com</a>:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Each internal system gets its own CN: [system-name].aiplus.perifery.com<=
/p></li>
<li>
<p>All services within a system are included as SANs</p></li>
<li>
<p>DNS managed through Route53</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Example for our lambda2 system:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>CN: <a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"http://lambda2.aiplus.perifery.co=
m" rel=3D"nofollow">lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com</a></p></li>
<li>
<p>SANs:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"http://notificationapi.lambda2.aiplus=
.perifery.com" rel=3D"nofollow">notificationapi.lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com=
</a></p></li>
<li>
<p><a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"http://metagen.lambda2.aiplus.perifer=
y.com" rel=3D"nofollow">metagen.lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com</a></p></li>
<li>
<p><a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"http://visageai.lambda2.aiplus.perife=
ry.com" rel=3D"nofollow">visageai.lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com</a></p></li>
<li>
<p>etc.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p>For customer systems, the domain structure is yet to be finalized. Howev=
er, we're strongly considering using our own domain structure in the form o=
f [customer-name].aiplus.perifery.com. This approach offers several advanta=
ges. First, it gives us complete control over DNS management and certificat=
e issuance/renewal, eliminating dependencies on customer DNS teams that cou=
ld delay updates. Second, using our domain simplifies our certificate manag=
ement infrastructure, allowing consistent processes across all deployments.=
 Finally, this standardization makes scaling our operations more straightfo=
rward, as we don't need to develop customer-specific certificate handling p=
rocesses.</p>
<p>Since we own the Perifery domain and have full control through Route53, =
we can easily automate Let's Encrypt certificate issuance and renewal using=
 the certbot DNS plugin for Route53. This provides a fully automated certif=
icate management solution without needing HTTP validation, which is particu=
larly useful for services that aren't publicly accessible.</p>
<p>While using customer domains might seem more "native" to their environme=
nts, the operational complexity it introduces often outweighs the benefits.=
 Our experience shows that certificate management is a significant operatio=
nal burden that's best kept under our control. Additionally, since our serv=
ices are typically accessed through customer applications rather than direc=
tly by end users, the domain names are largely transparent to users.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-Air-GappedEnvironment=
s">Air-Gapped Environments</h2>
<p>For deployment in air-gapped environments (such as government agencies),=
 special consideration will be needed during Phase 2 of our implementation.=
 When configuring Vault as a certificate authority, these environments pres=
ent unique challenges as they have no internet connectivity, making Let's E=
ncrypt certificate issuance impossible through standard methods.</p>
<p>For Phase 2 in air-gapped environments, we will need to adapt our Vault =
PKI implementation:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Customer-provided root/intermediate CA</strong>: Instead of usin=
g Let's Encrypt certificates as our intermediate CA in Vault, we would impo=
rt the customer's existing internal CA certificates. Vault would then issue=
 service certificates chained to their trusted root.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Offline CA initialization</strong>: We would initialize Vault's =
PKI engine using an offline process, generating and signing the intermediat=
e CA before deployment, then importing it into the air-gapped Vault instanc=
e.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>HSM integration with Vault</strong>: For high-security environme=
nts, Vault can be configured to use Hardware Security Modules for cryptogra=
phic operations, keeping private keys in tamper-resistant hardware.</p></li=
>
</ol>
<p>This specialized approach is not in scope for Phase 1 of our implementat=
ion, which focuses on standard environments with internet connectivity. The=
 air-gapped implementation details will be developed specifically for Phase=
 2 as needed for particular customer requirements.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-CertificateAcquisitio=
nandManagement">Certificate Acquisition and Management</h2>
<p>For our Phase 1 implementation, we obtain a single Let's Encrypt certifi=
cate containing all service domains as Subject Alternative Names:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">sudo ce=
rtbot certonly --standalone \
  --email admin@perifery.com \
  --agree-tos \
  -d lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com \
  -d notificationapi.lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com \
  -d metagen.lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com \
  # ... additional service domains
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>This approach simplifies certificate management while still providing pr=
oper TLS security for each service.</p>
<p>We automate certificate renewal using a simple cron job that:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>Attempts to renew certificates via certbot</p></li>
<li>
<p>Copies renewed certificates to a shared location</p></li>
<li>
<p>Distributes certificates to all servers</p></li>
<li>
<p>Signals services to reload certificates when necessary</p></li>
</ol>
<p>The renewal script runs monthly but only actually renews certificates wh=
en they're within 30 days of expiration.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-AI+Common">AI+ Common=
</h2>
<p>Our Common Library implementation abstracts certificate management and p=
rovides a consistent interface for enabling TLS/mTLS in all services.</p>
<p>We will create an abstract provider interface that decouples certificate=
 acquisition from usage. This separation allows us to swap implementation d=
etails without changing service code. In Phase 1, we use a simple provider =
that reads certificates from the filesystem.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: py; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Basic c=
ertificate provider interface
class CertificateProvider:
    def get_certificate(self):
        """Return tuple of (cert_path, key_path, ca_path)"""
        pass</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>In Phase 2, we'll use a provider that obtains certificates dynamically f=
rom Vault. This abstraction is key to our phased approach, allowing service=
s to transition gradually without code changes.<br><br></p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: py; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">class Vau=
ltCertificateProvider(CertificateProvider):
    def get_certificate(self):
        # We will authenticate to Vault and request certificate, etc...
        return (cert_path, key_path, ca_path)</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The TLS configuration handles certificate validation, SSL context creati=
on with secure defaults, TLS version management (supporting TLS 1.2/1.3), c=
ipher suite selection, and mTLS options for client certificate validation. =
We've implemented sane defaults that work out-of-the-box but can be customi=
zed when needed. The default TLS configurations are:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Minimum TLS version: 1.2</p></li>
<li>
<p>Preferred cipher suites: [List the suites] (Add the list of cipher suite=
s here)</p></li>
<li>
<p>Certificate validation: Enabled, using the system's trust store.</p></li=
>
</ul>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">def run=
_with_https(app, host=3D"0.0.0.0", port=3D8443):
    cert_provider =3D FileCertificateProvider()  # Or VaultCertificateProvi=
der
    cert_path, key_path, ca_path =3D cert_provider.get_certificate()
   =20
    uvicorn.run(
        app,=20
        host=3Dhost,=20
        port=3Dport,
        ssl_certfile=3Dcert_path,
        ssl_keyfile=3Dkey_path
    )</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The library provides simple integration with FastAPI applications throug=
h middleware for security headers (HSTS), Uvicorn configuration for HTTPS, =
and graceful fallback if certificates aren't available. The library handles=
 certificate loading/validation failures as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>If certificates are unavailable at startup: The service will log an erro=
r and may terminate, depending on a configuration setting. The default beha=
vior is to terminate.</p></li>
<li>
<p>If a certificate becomes invalid during runtime: The service will log an=
 error and attempt to reload. If reloading fails, the service will terminat=
e.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Our implementation specifically addresses mutual TLS requirements:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Server Certificate Validation: All services validate certificates presen=
ted by servers they connect to</p></li>
<li>
<p>Client Certificate Validation: Services can optionally require and valid=
ate client certificates. This is configured using the require_client_cert f=
lag in the TLS configuration. When set to True, the server will request and=
 validate client certificates.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Certificate Issuance Control: We control who can obtain valid certificat=
es</p></li>
</ul>
<p>This creates a zero-trust security model where both the client and serve=
r validate each other's identity.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-DockerIntegration">Do=
cker Integration</h2>
<p>In containerized environments, certificates need to be accessible inside=
 the containers while maintaining security. Here's our approach for each ph=
ase:</p>
<p>Phase 1: Filesystem-based Certificates</p>
<p>In Phase 1, we map the host certificate directory into containers as a r=
ead-only volume:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Conce=
ptual Docker Compose example
services:
  microservice:
    volumes:
      - /etc/aiplus/certs/shared:/app/certs:ro
    environment:
      - SSL_CERT_FILE=3D/app/certs/fullchain.pem
      - SSL_KEY_FILE=3D/app/certs/privkey.pem
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The /etc/aiplus/certs/shared directory on the host contains the shared c=
ertificates. This directory is mapped to /app/certs inside each container. =
The :ro flag ensures certificates are read-only within containers. Environm=
ent variables tell the application where to find the certificates. Certific=
ate renewal only requires updating files on the host. The distribution of t=
hese updated certificates to the hosts is handled by secure copy, or even T=
erraform. Access to the certificate directory on the host is restricted to =
the root user and the cert-manager group, with read-only permissions for th=
e group.</p>
<p>In Phase 2, containers will obtain certificates directly from Vault:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Conce=
ptual Docker Compose example for Phase 2
services:
  microservice:
    environment:
      - VAULT_ADDR=3Dhttps://vault.lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com:8200
      - VAULT_ROLE=3Dmicroservice
      # Service will use these to authenticate to Vault
      - VAULT_TOKEN_PATH=3D/run/secrets/vault-token
    secrets:
      - vault-token
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>In this approach, no certificate volumes are needed. Services authentica=
te to Vault and request certificates on startup. Certificates are stored in=
 memory or in ephemeral storage. Certificate renewal happens automatically =
through Vault. Each service receives a unique certificate appropriate for i=
ts identity.</p>
<p>The VAULT_TOKEN_PATH environment variable points to a file containing a =
Vault token. This token is provisioned as a Kubernetes secret (or Docker se=
cret, if not in Kubernetes) and mounted into the container. The token has a=
 limited lifetime and is rotated automatically using Vault Agent with auto-=
auth, leveraging a more secure authentication method such as AppRole.</p>
<p>Local Installation</p>
<p>I'll create a detailed wiki section on managing certificates for local T=
LS/mTLS development.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-LocalTLS/mTLSDevelopm=
ent">Local TLS/mTLS Development</h1>
<p>This section is strictly for configuring locally-run microservices to ac=
cess Let's Encrypt certificates on a development environment. These steps e=
nsure TLS/mTLS functions properly during local development while using the =
same production certificates.</p>
<div class=3D"confluence-information-macro confluence-information-macro-inf=
ormation">
<span class=3D"aui-icon aui-icon-small aui-iconfont-info confluence-informa=
tion-macro-icon"></span>
<div class=3D"confluence-information-macro-body">
<p>Note that this section applies ONLY to <strong>development</strong>. We =
should not be changing groups or ownership in production!</p>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-CertificateAccessConf=
iguration">Certificate Access Configuration</h2>
<p>The easiest way to get access to the certs is to change the group owners=
hip. On the Lambda machines, there is a group called =E2=80=9Caiplus=E2=80=
=9D we use for managing access to shared resources.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Creat=
e a dedicated group for certificate access (if it doesn't exist)
sudo groupadd aiplus</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We will need to configure Directory and File Permissions</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Set p=
roper group permissions on certificate directories
sudo chmod 755 /etc/letsencrypt
sudo chmod 755 /etc/letsencrypt/live
sudo chmod 750 /etc/letsencrypt/archive

# Set group ownership
sudo chgrp aiplus /etc/letsencrypt/live
sudo chgrp -R aiplus /etc/letsencrypt/archive/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com

# Make certificate files readable by the group
sudo chmod -R g+rx /etc/letsencrypt/archive/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com

# Ensure private keys have restricted permissions
sudo chmod 640 /etc/letsencrypt/archive/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com/privkey=
1.pem

# Set execute bit on necessary directories to make symlinks traversable
sudo chmod g+x /etc/letsencrypt/archive
sudo chmod g+x /etc/letsencrypt/archive/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Make sure the username is added to the group that owns the certificate.<=
/p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Add y=
our user to the certificate access group
sudo usermod -a -G aiplus &lt;username&gt;
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>In Consul, we will need to update each service's configuration to use th=
e Let's Encrypt certificates:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">tls:
  enabled: true
  provider_type: file
  certificate:
    file:
      cert_dir: /etc/letsencrypt/live/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com
      cert_file: fullchain.pem
      key_file: privkey.pem
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-HostnameConfiguration=
">Hostname Configuration</h2>
<p>If the CN and SANs do not map to an IP address, accessing the FastAPI pa=
ge on the machine will produce security warnings. To address these certific=
ate name validation issues, the easiest thing to do is map the IP address t=
o the host.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Add e=
ntries to /etc/hosts
sudo nano /etc/hosts

# Add lines like:
127.0.0.1 lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com
127.0.0.1 asr-service.lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com
127.0.0.1 vlm-service.lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com
# Add all relevant service hostnames
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We can also update the service configuration to use the hostnames that m=
atch the certificate:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Examp=
le configuration
config.update({
    "service": {
        "host": "asr-service.lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com",
        "port": 8443
    }
})
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-TroubleshootingCertif=
icateAccess">Troubleshooting Certificate Access</h2>
<p>Here=E2=80=99s some simple and easy steps we can take if we encounter de=
bugging issues.</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Verify group membership</strong>:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">groups =
&lt;username&gt;
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Ensure <code>aiplus</code> appears in the list. You may need to log out =
and back in for group changes to take effect.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Check file ownership and permissions</strong>:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">ls -la =
/etc/letsencrypt/live/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com/
ls -la /etc/letsencrypt/archive/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com/
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Verify group ownership and permissions are correct.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Test certificate accessibility</strong>:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">openssl=
 x509 -in /etc/letsencrypt/live/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com/fullchain.pem -=
text -noout
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>This command should run without permission errors.</p></li>
</ol>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-CertificateRenewalCon=
siderations">Certificate Renewal Considerations</h2>
<p>When Let's Encrypt certificates are renewed:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>The symlinks in <code>/etc/letsencrypt/live/</code> will point to new fi=
les</p></li>
<li>
<p>We need to ensure the renewal process maintains the correct permissions<=
/p></li>
<li>
<p>We should add a post-renewal hook to fix permissions:</p></li>
</ol>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Add t=
o /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/post/fix-permissions.sh
#!/bin/bash
chgrp -R aiplus /etc/letsencrypt/archive/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com
chmod -R g+rx /etc/letsencrypt/archive/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com
chmod 640 /etc/letsencrypt/archive/lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com/privkey*.pem
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-ConsulandVaultTLSConf=
iguration">Consul and Vault TLS Configuration</h2>
<p>Consul requires certificates for both its server-to-server and client-to=
-server communications. Our approach is to mount certificates into Consul c=
ontainers, configure TLS settings to require verification for both incoming=
 and outgoing connections, disable HTTP endpoints (forcing all API communic=
ations over HTTPS), and enable Consul Connect with TLS for service mesh cap=
abilities.</p>
<p>Verify both incoming and outgoing connections, use the shared certificat=
e for server identification, secure RPC, HTTP and gossip protocols, and set=
 minimum TLS version to 1.2.</p>
<p>Consul Connect will use the certificates to establish mTLS connections b=
etween services. Each service will present its certificate to other service=
s, and verify the certificates of the services it connects to, based on the=
 service identity provided by Consul.</p>
<p>Vault's TLS configuration is similar but includes additional settings fo=
r its role as a certificate authority. We secure the Vault API and UI with =
TLS, configure the PKI secrets engine for certificate issuance (Phase 2), s=
et up certificate roles that map to service types, and implement authentica=
tion methods for services to obtain certificates.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-VaultPKIImplementatio=
n">Vault PKI Implementation</h2>
<p>In Phase 2, we'll leverage Vault's PKI secrets engine to enhance our cer=
tificate management.</p>
<p>For initial setup, we configure Vault's PKI secrets engine, set up a cer=
tificate authority hierarchy, and define certificate roles and policies.</p=
>
<p>To use our Let's Encrypt certificate as an intermediate CA in Vault:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># 1. Im=
port the Let's Encrypt certificate chain and private key into Vault
vault write pki/config/ca \
  pem_bundle=3D@/etc/aiplus/certs/shared/fullchain-and-key.pem

# 2. Set up a role for issuing service certificates
vault write pki/roles/service-role \
  allowed_domains=3D"lambda2.aiplus.perifery.com,*.lambda2.aiplus.perifery.=
com" \
  allow_subdomains=3Dtrue \
  max_ttl=3D"72h"</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The imported Let's Encrypt certificate serves as an intermediate CA that=
 Vault uses to sign service certificates. This maintains the trust chain ba=
ck to Let's Encrypt while allowing Vault to dynamically issue certificates.=
 Since Let's Encrypt certificates expire after 90 days, we'll need to updat=
e the Vault CA configuration when we renew our Let's Encrypt certificates.<=
/p>
<p>For service integration, services authenticate to Vault using appropriat=
e credentials, request certificates with specific parameters (CN, SANs, TTL=
), Vault issues short-lived certificates (24-72 hours), and services automa=
tically renew certificates before expiration.</p>
<p>The advantages of this approach include dynamic certificate issuance, au=
tomatic rotation, fine-grained access control, comprehensive audit logging,=
 and shorter certificate lifetimes for enhanced security.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-356:AI+SystemTLS/mTLSImplementationGuide-KubernetesMigration">=
Kubernetes Migration</h2>
<p>When transitioning to Kubernetes, our TLS implementation will evolve:</p=
>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Certificate Storage: Using Kubernetes Secrets instead of filesystem</p><=
/li>
<li>
<p>Certificate Distribution: Leveraging Kubernetes mechanisms</p></li>
<li>
<p>Vault Integration: Using Kubernetes auth methods for Vault integration</=
p></li>
</ul>
<p>This will be detailed further when we begin Kubernetes migration plannin=
g.</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
